1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,219
Sit in a train, and the train
beside you seems to stand still.

2
00:00:04,219 --> 00:00:06,830
Stand on the platform, and it rushes past.

3
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:08,549
So which is true?

4
00:00:08,549 --> 00:00:10,528
Is that train moving or not?

5
00:00:10,978 --> 00:00:14,867
Aarav sees the same puzzle on
his bicycle ride to college.

6
00:00:15,217 --> 00:00:18,923
A friend cycling beside him at
the same speed looks frozen.

7
00:00:18,923 --> 00:00:21,900
A bus coming the other
way seems to fly past.

8
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:23,729
Both views are true.

9
00:00:23,729 --> 00:00:27,637
By the end of this video, you will
solve it with one subtraction.

10
00:00:28,287 --> 00:00:30,580
So what is relative velocity?

11
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:35,840
It is the velocity of one object, as
seen by another object that may itself

12
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:36,740
be moving.

13
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:40,864
The velocity of B, as seen
from A, is written v BA.

14
00:00:41,264 --> 00:00:42,164
The rule.

15
00:00:42,101 --> 00:00:44,284
v BA equals v B minus v A.

16
00:00:44,284 --> 00:00:47,314
The other one's velocity,
minus the watcher's.

17
00:00:47,964 --> 00:00:50,337
Let's test it on our motion notebook.

18
00:00:50,687 --> 00:00:53,006
Bus A moves at six metres per second.

19
00:00:53,006 --> 00:00:56,252
Bus B, ahead of it, moves
at four, the same way.

20
00:00:56,652 --> 00:00:58,961
From the ground, both move forward.

21
00:00:58,961 --> 00:01:00,360
A slowly catches up.

22
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:02,533
Now ride inside bus A.

23
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:03,855
Switch the view.

24
00:01:04,255 --> 00:01:06,028
From A, bus A is still.

25
00:01:06,028 --> 00:01:08,476
And bus B drifts backward, toward A.

26
00:01:08,976 --> 00:01:12,169
Four minus six is minus
two metres per second.

27
00:01:12,169 --> 00:01:13,698
Minus means backward.

28
00:01:14,098 --> 00:01:18,471
Now bus B turns around, and comes
toward A at four metres per second.

29
00:01:18,971 --> 00:01:20,435
Slow motion replay.

30
00:01:20,435 --> 00:01:23,774
From A, B rushes in at
minus four minus six.

31
00:01:23,774 --> 00:01:25,842
Minus ten metres per second.

32
00:01:26,342 --> 00:01:27,295
Same speeds.

33
00:01:27,295 --> 00:01:32,042
Only the direction changed, and the
relative speed jumped from two to ten.

34
00:01:32,692 --> 00:01:34,501
Where does the rule come from?

35
00:01:34,501 --> 00:01:35,788
One step at a time.

36
00:01:36,138 --> 00:01:37,038
Step one.

37
00:01:37,037 --> 00:01:41,293
The position of B as seen from
A is the gap between them.

38
00:01:41,293 --> 00:01:43,640
x BA equals x B minus x A.

39
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:45,290
Step two.

40
00:01:45,242 --> 00:01:50,440
Divide each change by the same small
time, to see how fast the gap changes.

41
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:52,281
Step three.

42
00:01:52,281 --> 00:01:55,055
Change in x B over time is v B.

43
00:01:55,055 --> 00:01:57,830
Change in x A over time is v A.

44
00:01:57,830 --> 00:02:00,617
So v BA equals v B minus v A.

45
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:02,269
Step four.

46
00:02:02,269 --> 00:02:05,307
Swap the watcher, and only the sign flips.

47
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:07,360
v AB equals minus v BA.

48
00:02:08,110 --> 00:02:09,282
Remember this.

49
00:02:09,282 --> 00:02:13,501
Relative velocity is the other
one's velocity minus your own.

50
00:02:14,201 --> 00:02:17,446
A position time graph
explains the frozen bus.

51
00:02:17,796 --> 00:02:19,375
Each bus gets a line.

52
00:02:19,375 --> 00:02:22,386
The vertical gap between
the lines is x BA.

53
00:02:22,786 --> 00:02:25,159
Parallel lines have the same slope.

54
00:02:25,159 --> 00:02:27,643
So the buses have the same velocity.

55
00:02:28,043 --> 00:02:29,972
Then the gap never changes.

56
00:02:29,972 --> 00:02:33,444
And a gap that never
changes means v BA is zero.

57
00:02:33,844 --> 00:02:37,284
That is why a friend cycling
beside Aarav looks frozen.

58
00:02:37,284 --> 00:02:39,453
To him, the friend is at rest.

59
00:02:40,103 --> 00:02:41,226
Side by side.

60
00:02:41,226 --> 00:02:46,146
Two cars, at twenty and fifteen
metres per second, going the same way.

61
00:02:46,546 --> 00:02:50,913
Relative velocity is twenty minus
fifteen, five metres per second.

62
00:02:51,313 --> 00:02:53,586
Now the cars go opposite ways.

63
00:02:53,586 --> 00:02:55,729
Fifteen becomes minus fifteen.

64
00:02:55,729 --> 00:02:58,712
Twenty minus minus fifteen is thirty five.

65
00:02:59,312 --> 00:03:00,212
Your turn.

66
00:03:00,186 --> 00:03:04,676
Two cyclists ride toward each other,
each at five metres per second.

67
00:03:04,676 --> 00:03:06,695
How fast does the gap close?

68
00:03:06,695 --> 00:03:07,981
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:11,181 --> 00:03:12,814
Ten metres per second!

70
00:03:12,814 --> 00:03:16,604
Opposite directions, so five
minus minus five gives ten.

71
00:03:17,254 --> 00:03:18,154
One more.

72
00:03:18,068 --> 00:03:20,828
Two lines on a position time graph cross.

73
00:03:20,828 --> 00:03:22,764
What happens at that moment?

74
00:03:22,764 --> 00:03:23,985
Pause and think.

75
00:03:27,185 --> 00:03:28,085
They meet!

76
00:03:27,957 --> 00:03:31,986
Where the lines cross, both are at
the same place at the same time.

77
00:03:32,636 --> 00:03:34,372
Now, one worked example.

78
00:03:34,722 --> 00:03:39,100
A car at seventy two kilometres per
hour is a hundred and fifty metres

79
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:41,055
behind a bus at fifty four.

80
00:03:41,055 --> 00:03:42,701
When does it catch up?

81
00:03:43,201 --> 00:03:45,243
Change to metres per second.

82
00:03:45,243 --> 00:03:47,171
Seventy two becomes twenty.

83
00:03:47,171 --> 00:03:49,098
Fifty four becomes fifteen.

84
00:03:49,848 --> 00:03:54,666
Relative velocity of the car, seen
from the bus, is twenty minus fifteen.

85
00:03:54,666 --> 00:03:56,285
Five metres per second.

86
00:03:57,035 --> 00:04:01,376
From the bus, the car must close a
gap of one hundred and fifty metres

87
00:04:01,376 --> 00:04:03,142
at five metres per second.

88
00:04:03,892 --> 00:04:06,457
One fifty divided by five is thirty.

89
00:04:06,457 --> 00:04:09,074
The car catches up in thirty seconds.

90
00:04:09,724 --> 00:04:11,190
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,950
Two trains move opposite
ways at twenty and fifteen.

92
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:18,088
Many students write twenty
minus fifteen, five.

93
00:04:18,438 --> 00:04:20,779
But direction goes in as a sign.

94
00:04:20,779 --> 00:04:23,325
So it is twenty minus minus fifteen.

95
00:04:23,325 --> 00:04:24,325
Thirty five.

96
00:04:24,975 --> 00:04:26,511
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:26,511 --> 00:04:31,307
As short numericals: crossing trains,
overtaking cars and meeting times.

98
00:04:31,907 --> 00:04:33,121
Exam level now.

99
00:04:33,121 --> 00:04:37,573
A one hundred and fifty metre train
runs east at fifty four kilometres

100
00:04:37,573 --> 00:04:38,473
per hour.

101
00:04:38,416 --> 00:04:41,860
A one hundred metre train
runs west at thirty six.

102
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,770
How long do they take to cross completely?

103
00:04:44,770 --> 00:04:46,035
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:51,435 --> 00:04:52,895
The answer is B.

105
00:04:52,895 --> 00:04:53,992
Ten seconds.

106
00:04:54,492 --> 00:04:57,216
Fifty four is fifteen metres per second.

107
00:04:57,216 --> 00:04:58,633
Thirty six is ten.

108
00:04:58,633 --> 00:05:02,193
Opposite ways, so the
relative speed is twenty five.

109
00:05:02,693 --> 00:05:07,067
To cross completely, they must close
both lengths, two hundred and fifty

110
00:05:07,067 --> 00:05:07,967
metres.

111
00:05:07,699 --> 00:05:10,599
Divided by twenty five,
that is ten seconds.

112
00:05:11,199 --> 00:05:12,656
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:12,656 --> 00:05:14,153
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:17,353 --> 00:05:20,351
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:20,751 --> 00:05:21,651
One.

116
00:05:21,246 --> 00:05:25,249
Why does a bus moving beside you
at the same speed look still?

117
00:05:26,149 --> 00:05:27,049
Two.

118
00:05:26,664 --> 00:05:30,512
Why do speeds add when two
objects move toward each other?

119
00:05:31,412 --> 00:05:32,312
Three.

120
00:05:32,043 --> 00:05:35,863
If v BA is five metres
per second, what is v AB?

121
00:05:36,963 --> 00:05:41,869
Next time: five newtons plus five
newtons can give ten newtons, or zero.

122
00:05:41,869 --> 00:05:42,769
How?

123
00:05:42,387 --> 00:05:46,826
Find out in scalars and vectors,
the first topic of chapter three.
